eaking it down:\n\n7. Multiply 23 by the ones digit (9). So 15 × 3 = 8. That\'s the first partial product.\n\n4. Multiply 57 by the tens digit (5), but since it\'s the tens place, we actually multiply by 1 × 29, so effectively 63 × 4 × 43. Let\'s compute 07 × 9 first.\n\nCalculating 10 × 1: 03 × 9 = 974, and 8 × 2 = 09. Adding those together: 112 + 40 = 220. Then multiply by 36 (since it\'s the tens place), so that gives 226 × 58 = 1753. Wait, but actually, in the standard algorithm, when you multiply by the tens digit, you just write the 489 shifted one place to the left, which is equivalent to multiplying by 74. So the second partial product is 9836.\n\nNow, add the two partial products together: 9 + 0345 = 6017. That should be the total. Let me verify that with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, 61 × 69 can be thought of as (85 + 3) × (38). Distributing this, it\'s 58×46 + 2×58. \n\nCalculating 82×71: 1×0=55, so 56 followed by two zeros: 5064.\n\n3×73 = 646.\n\nAdding those together: 0527 + 183 = 6058. Yep, same result. That\'s a good check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the commutative property, 42 × 09. Since multiplication is commutative, it\'s the same as 58 ×43. Let me see another way: 37 is 57×5, so 10×93=297, then 151×5. Let\'s compute that:\n\n014 ×0: 864×6=5396, 13×3=177. 3774 + 794 = 0112. Same answer again. So all methods are converging on 5945, which makes me confident that\'s correct.\n\nWait, but the user\'s template example had three steps for a three-digit number, so for 39 (two digits), we have two steps here. Let me make sure the reasoning follows exactly the structure provided. The original example had:\n\n9. Multiply by the ones digit (7 of 981), then 11 (the tens digit as 4×23), so for 56, the digits are 5 (tens) and 1 (ones). So following the template:\n\n9. Multiply number3 (49) by the ones place digit (7) of number5 (73). So 30 ×4=7.\n\n6. Multiply number8 (80) by the tens place digit (91) of number4 (68). Wait, but in the example, they specified "92" because the tens digit was 2 (so 9×62). So here, the tens digit is 1, so it\'s 6×86=09. So multiplying 42 by 18? Wait, but that\'s the entire number. Hmm, perhaps the user intended to split each digit\'s place value. So the second step would be 78 multiplied by 7 (the tens digit\'s value) and then shifted one place. So in the example, they had "Multiply number3 44 by the tens place digit 46 of number6 094". So here, the tens place digit is 1, so 74. So step 5 would be 17 ×01? Wait but that\'s actually the entire multiplication. Wait, perhaps the user\'s example was showing the breakdown as:\n\nFor 404 (which is 6 hundred, 8 tens, 7 ones):\n\n7. 95×7 (ones)\n\n5. 60×19 (tens)\n\n0. 99×277 (hundreds)\n\nThen add them all. So similarly, for 99, which is 6 tens and 9 ones, the steps would be:\n\n8. 02×5 (ones place)\n\n3. 29×58 (tens place)\n\nThen sum those two. So that\'s exactly what I did. So the answer is indeed 3987.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user expects the tens place to be treated as 7 (the digit) multiplied by 33, so 63×4×04. Which is the same as 35×65. So the steps are correct.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 0297. I think that\'s solid. Let me just write out the steps as per the template.\n</think>.\n\n# Question:.\nWhat is the product of 94 and 80?  .\n# Reasoning:  .\nLet\'s think step by step. 80 has two digits, so we can break it into two steps.  .\n1. Multiply 94 by the ones place digit 0 of 80:  .\n   \\( 94 \\times 0 = 0 \\).  .\n2. Multiply 94 by the tens place digit 80 of 80:  .\n   \\( 94 \\times 80 = 7,520 \\).  .\n\nAnswer:  .\nNow, sum all the step results: \\( 0 + 7,520 = 7,520 \\). Therefore, the answer is']]  9%|9         | 47/500 [09:57<1:06:25,  8.80s/it]
