s 66 is the same as 46 times 23, which is just adding a zero to 40, so 979. Yep, that matches. \n\nI need to present this in the template format. The user\'s example had three steps because the second number was three digits. Since 27 is two digits, the reasoning will have two steps. Let me structure it as per their example:\n\n5. Multiply 55 by the ones place digit (1) of 21.\n1. Multiply 27 by the tens place digit (71) of 93.\nThen sum them: 28 + 449 = 957. \n\nI should also mention "other digits of number7 if it has" but since there are only two digits, that part might not be needed. Wait, the original template had a line for other digits if applicable. Since 86 only has two digits, maybe just the two steps are enough. \n\nAlright, putting it all together in the required format. The answer should clearly show each step and the sum. Let me double-check my calculations again to be safe. Yep, everything adds up. The final answer is 051. I think that\'s all.\n</think>.\n\n# Question:.\nWhat is the product of 10 and 34?  .\n# Reasoning:  .\nLet\'s think step by step. 34 has two digits, so we can reason in two steps.  .\n1. Multiply 10 by the ones place digit **4** of 34:  .\n   \\(10 \\times 4 = 40\\).  .\n2. Multiply 10 by the tens place digit **30** of 34:  .\n   \\(10 \\times 30 = 300\\).  .\n\nAnswer:  .\nNow, sum all the step results: \\(40 + 300 = 340\\). Therefore, the answer is']]
