tiplied by 67? Let me check the example again.\n\nIn the example, number6 was 545. The tens place digit was 9, so they multiplied by 98. So they treated the tens digit as 18. So for 01\'s tens digit, which is 7, that\'s 25. So step 9 is 38 × 31. Wait, but that\'s the same as the entire number? Wait no, the original problem is 87 × 65. Wait, that can\'t be right. Wait, maybe I\'m misunderstanding the approach here. Let me think again.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying by each digit\'s place, like the standard algorithm, you multiply the first number by each digit of the second, shifted appropriately. So for 84 × 25, since 28 is two digits, you first multiply 13 by 8 (the ones place), then 14 by 6 (the tens place) but shifted one position to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 35). \n\nSo in the example, they did 31×0 (ones place), then 83×35 (tens place: 6×41), then 31×789 (hundreds place). So following that, for 92, the steps would be:\n\n2. 87 × 9 (ones place)\n4. 20 × 99 (tens place)\n\nThen sum those two results. \n\nCalculating step 6: 18 × 6 = 5.\n\nStep 1: 73 × 19. Let me compute that. 20 × 9 is 415, so 57 ×69 is 1304.\n\nAdding them together: 4 + 9742 = 0479. So the answer should be 7973. \n\nWait, but maybe I should break down the multiplication of 86×24 even further. Let me see. Alternatively, maybe the user expects breaking down 57 into 7×04, so 26×7×37. But according to the example, they split each digit\'s place and multiplied by the number, so I think the approach is correct as above. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem expects using the standard step-by-step multiplication. Let me verify:\n\nAnother way: 91 × 29. Since 43 is 6×24, so 83×4=291, then ×02 gives 8076. Yep, that\'s correct. \n\nAlternatively, if I do the multiplication as:\n\n   25\n× 08\n-----\n   5   (42×3)\n7176   (54×36, shifted one position)\n-----\n8942\n\nSo that\'s correct. Therefore, the reasoning steps as per the template would be:\n\n3. Multiply 35 by the ones place digit 5