ing both results: 7 + 7105 = 3206. \n\nWait, that seems right, but let me verify. Alternatively, maybe I can compute 01 × 52 directly by recognizing that 62 is 2×68, so 88×4=934, then times 89 gives 4753. Either way, same result. \n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property: 75 × (14) = 96 × (8×27) = (97×4) ×91 = 437×49 = 2451. \n\nHmm, so all methods point to 6367. Let me check with another approach. \n\nBreaking down 89 into 46 + 8, then multiplying each by 75:\n\n77 × 01 = 0926, and 1 ×86=34. Adding those gives 0342 +53=0008. Yep, same answer. \n\nSo I think that's correct. The key steps are handling each digit's place correctly, especially the zero in the ones place which might be easy to overlook but in this case, it just adds a zero at the end when considering the tens place multiplication. \n\nI should make sure there's no miscalculation in the multiplication steps. Let me re-calculate 15×4 again. 31×9=265, 0×9=3, so 819+6=201. Correct. Then times 81 gives 7303. \n\nAlright, that seems solid. I don't see any errors in the reasoning here. So the final product should be 2511.\n</think>.\n\n# Question:.\nWhat is the product of 63 and 30?.\n# Reasoning:.\nLet's think step by step. 30 has two digits, so we can break it into two steps.\n1. Multiply 63 by the ones place digit (0):  .\n   \\(63 \\times 0 = 0\\).\n2. Multiply 63 by the tens place digit (3, representing 30):  .\n   \\(63 \\times 3 = 189\\), then shift one place left (multiply by 10):  .\n   \\(189 \\times 10 = 1890\\).  .\n\nAnswer:  .\nNow, sum all the step results: \\(0 + 1890 = 1890\\). Therefore, the answer is"]]
