l 511. Then \u00d710 gives 5110.\n\nNow, sum the two results: 292 + 5110 = 5402.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm:\n\n   73  \n\u00d774  \n------  \nMultiply 73 by 4: 292\n\nMultiply 73 by 70 (shift one position to the left):\n\n73\u00d77=511, so 5110.\n\nAdding 292 + 5110:\n\n292 + 5110 = 5402.\n\nAlternatively, check via (70 +3)(70 +4) =70\u00d770 +70\u00d74 +3\u00d770 +3\u00d74 =4900 +280 +210 +12= 4900+280=5180; 5180+210=5390; 5390+12=5402. Correct.\n\nSo the answer should be 5402.\n\nTherefore, the reasoning steps should be:\n\n1. Multiply 73 by 4 (the ones digit of 74) to get 292.\n\n2. Multiply 73 by 70 (the tens digit 7, times 10) to get 5110.\n\nSum those two results: 292 + 5110 = 5402.\n\nTherefore, the final product is 5402.\n\nWait, but the user's template example had three steps for a three-digit number. Since 74 is two digits, the steps are two.\n\nAlright, so I should structure the answer accordingly.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 73 and 74?\n\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 74 has two digits, so we can reason in two steps.  \n1. Multiply 73 by the ones place digit 4 of 74:  \n   \\( 73 \\times 4 = 292 \\).  \n2. Multiply 73 by the tens place digit 70 (since 7 is in the tens place, it represents \\( 7 \\times 10 \\)):  \n   \\( 73 \\times 70 = 5110 \\).  \n\nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\( 292 + 5110 = 5402 \\).  \nSo, the final computed product is **5402**.",
