y the ones place digit 3 of 53: 61 × 3 = 183\n2. Multiply 61 by the tens place digit 50 of 53: 61 × 50 = 3050\nAnswer:\nNow, sum all the step results: 183 + 3050 = 3233.\nSo, the final computed product is 3233.\n\nYes, that\'s correct. I need to make sure I didn\'t make any arithmetic errors here. 61×50 is indeed 3050, and 61×3 is 183. Adding them gives 3233. Correct.\n\nEarlier confusion was due to mixing up with different numbers in my head, but now it\'s clear. The key is to follow the steps as per the template, breaking the second number into its digits and multiplying each by the first number, then summing.\n\nAnother way to verify is using (60+1)(50+3) = 60×50 + 60×3 + 1×50 + 1×3 = 3000 + 180 + 50 + 3 = 3000+180=3180, 3180+50=3230, +3=3233. Yep, same result. So confident now.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 61 and 53?\n# Reasoning:\nLet\'s think step by step. 53 has two digits, so we can reason in two steps.\n1. Multiply 61 by the ones place digit 3 of 53:  \n   \\(61 \\times 3 = 183\\)  \n2. Multiply 61 by the tens place digit 50 of 53:  \n   \\(61 \\times 50 = 3050\\)  \n\nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\(183 + 3050 = 3233\\).  \nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{3233}.', '']
